A <parametrization> of the straight <line segment> is $\mathbf r(t)=(t,t,t)$, $0\le t\le1$. Then $\mathbf F(\mathbf r(t))=(t,t^2,t)$ and $\mathbf r'(t)=(1,1,1)$. By the <line integral> formula,
$$
\boxed{\int_C\mathbf F\cdot d\mathbf r
=\int_0^1(2t+t^2)\,dt=\frac43.}
$$

= ii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}
{c}

Evaluate the <line integral> separately on the three oriented <line segments>. Their <parametrizations> and contributions are
$$
\begin{array}{c|c|c}
\mathbf r(t),\ 0\le t\le1&\mathbf F(\mathbf r(t))\cdot\mathbf r'(t)&\text{contribution}\\\hline
(t,0,0)&0&0\\
(1,t,0)&0&0\\
(1,1,t)&t&1/2
\end{array}
$$
Thus
$$
\boxed{\int_C\mathbf F\cdot d\mathbf r=\frac12.}
$$
The different value in <3/b/i> demonstrates <path dependence>. The <vector field> in this part is different from the <conservative vector field> in part (a).

