= Solution
{parent=i}
{c}

Use the ordered outcomes of the two rolls as the <sample space>:
$$
\boxed{\Omega=\{1,\ldots,6\}^2,\quad\mathcal F=2^\Omega,\quad
\mathbb P(E)=\frac{|E|}{36}\quad(E\subseteq\Omega).}
$$
Thus each elementary outcome has <probability> $1/36$. This is the usual <probability space> for two independent fair dice.

= ii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}
{c}

For an outcome $\omega=(i,j)$, the two <random variables> are the coordinate maps
$$
\boxed{X(i,j)=i,\qquad Y(i,j)=j.}
$$
Their <marginal probability mass functions> are $p_X(i)=p_Y(j)=1/6$ on $\{1,\ldots,6\}$, and their <joint probability mass function> satisfies
$$
p_{X,Y}(i,j)=\frac1{36}=p_X(i)p_Y(j).
$$
Off that support, the same factorization holds with zeros. Hence **$X$ and $Y$ are <independent random variables>**, by the <joint probability mass function> criterion for <independence>.

= iii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}
{c}

There are three even faces, so $\mathbb P(A)=1/2$. The sum is even precisely when both faces have the same <parity>, giving $9+9=18$ outcomes and $\mathbb P(B)=1/2$. Both $A$ and $B$ occur precisely when both faces are even, giving nine outcomes. Therefore
$$
\boxed{\mathbb P(A\cap B)=\frac9{36}=\frac14
=\mathbb P(A)\mathbb P(B).}
$$
Thus $A$ and $B$ are <independent events>.

= iv
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iv}
{c}

**No: <pairwise independence> does not imply that the three are <independent events>.** Take $C=\{Y\text{ is even}\}$. Then $\mathbb P(C)=1/2$, and $\mathbb P(A\cap C)=9/36=1/4$ by <independence> of the two rolls. Also $B\cap C$ is precisely the event that both faces are even, so $\mathbb P(B\cap C)=1/4$. Thus all three pairs of <events> are <independent>.

But $A\cap B\cap C$ also consists of the nine even-even outcomes, so
$$
\boxed{\mathbb P(A\cap B\cap C)=\frac14\ne\frac18
=\mathbb P(A)\mathbb P(B)\mathbb P(C).}
$$
This violates the triple-intersection requirement for <independent events>.

