**The printed numerical example reverses the digits and contradicts both definitions.** With the stated last-digit definition, $[Z]=17$ gives $X=7$, $Y=1$. Use those definitions throughout.

Put $q=e^{-\lambda}\in(0,1)$ and $N=\lfloor Z\rfloor$. The <exponential distribution> gives
$$
\mathbb P(N=n)=\mathbb P(n\le Z<n+1)=(1-q)q^n,\qquad n\ge0.
$$
The <Euclidean division> $N=10Y+X$ has unique remainder $X\in\{0,\ldots,9\}$ and quotient $Y\in\{0,1,\ldots\}$. Thus the <joint probability mass function> is
$$
\boxed{p_{X,Y}(x,y)=(1-q)q^{x+10y},\quad
x=0,\ldots,9,\ y=0,1,\ldots,}
$$
and zero otherwise. This is <decimal digit decomposition of a geometric random variable>. Following the erroneous example instead would require different definitions and would not answer the stated question.

= ii
{parent=c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}
{c}

Summing the <joint probability mass function> over $y$ with the <geometric series> gives the <marginal probability mass function>
$$
p_X(x)=\frac{(1-q)q^x}{1-q^{10}},\qquad x=0,\ldots,9.
$$
Divide the <joint probability mass function> by this positive <marginal probability mass function> to obtain the <conditional distribution>:
$$
\boxed{\mathbb P(Y=y\mid X=x)=(1-q^{10})q^{10y},\qquad y\ge0,\quad0\le x\le9.}
$$
It is the <geometric distribution> counting failures before the first success, with success <probability> $1-q^{10}$. For the other nonnegative integers $x\ge10$, $\mathbb P(X=x)=0$; the elementary <conditional probability> given $X=x$ is undefined and supplies no additional case.

= iii
{parent=c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}
{c}

Summing over the last digit yields the <marginal probability mass function>
$$
p_Y(y)=(1-q)q^{10y}\sum_{x=0}^9q^x=(1-q^{10})q^{10y},\qquad y\ge0.
$$
Together with the other <marginal probability mass function>, this gives
$$
\boxed{p_{X,Y}(x,y)=p_X(x)p_Y(y).}
$$
The factorization holds on the whole support and with zeros elsewhere, proving that **$X$ and $Y$ are <independent>**. The <conditional distribution> in <4/c/ii> likewise does not depend on the permissible value $x$.

