= i
{parent=c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}
{c}

An <expected value> of a signed <random variable> is finite precisely when its absolute value is <integrable>. With the <standard normal distribution> <probability density function>,
$$
\mathbb E|Ze^{\alpha Z^2}|
=\frac2{\sqrt{2\pi}}\int_0^\infty z\exp\bigl[-(1/2-\alpha)z^2\bigr]\,dz.
$$
For $\alpha<1/2$, this <integral> equals $1/[\sqrt{2\pi}(1/2-\alpha)]<\infty$. For $\alpha\ge1/2$, its <integrand> does not decay sufficiently and the <integral> diverges. Therefore
$$
\boxed{\mathbb E[Ze^{\alpha Z^2}]\text{ exists exactly for }\alpha<\frac12,
\quad\text{and then equals }0.}
$$
The value is zero by the <odd function> and the <symmetric distribution>. For the other $\alpha$, both positive and negative parts have infinite <integrals>; formal cancellation would give only a <Cauchy principal value>, not an <expected value>. This distinction is the <integrability threshold for exponential powers of a Gaussian>.

= ii
{parent=c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}
{c}

Absolute <integrability> now requires
$$
\mathbb E|Ze^{\alpha Z^4}|
=\frac2{\sqrt{2\pi}}\int_0^\infty z e^{\alpha z^4-z^2/2}\,dz<\infty.
$$
For $\alpha\le0$, the <integrand> is bounded by $ze^{-z^2/2}$, whose <integral> is finite. For $\alpha>0$, once $z^2\ge1/\alpha$, the exponent is at least $\alpha z^4/2$, so the <integral> diverges. Hence
$$
\boxed{\mathbb E[Ze^{\alpha Z^4}]\text{ exists exactly for }\alpha\le0,
\quad\text{and then equals }0.}
$$
Again the zero follows from the <odd function> and <symmetry>; outside the <integrable> range both signed parts diverge, so the <expected value> is undefined.

= iii
{parent=c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}
{c}

Let $A=\{|Z|>2\}$ and $B=\{Z>0\}$. The <standard normal distribution> is symmetric and assigns no <probability mass> to zero. Thus $\mathbb P(B)=1/2$, and its equal two-sided tails give
$$
\mathbb P(A)=2\mathbb P(Z>2),\qquad
\mathbb P(A\cap B)=\mathbb P(Z>2).
$$
Therefore
$$
\boxed{\mathbb P(A\cap B)=\mathbb P(A)\mathbb P(B),}
$$
so **the two <events> are <independent>**. The permitted approximation gives $\mathbb P(A)\approx0.05$ and $\mathbb P(A\cap B)\approx0.025$, but <independence> is exact.

