A <joint probability density function> integrates to one. Here
$$
1=C\int_0^1\int_0^1u(1+2v)\,dv\,du
=C\left(\frac12\right)(2),
$$
so
$$
\boxed{C=1.}
$$
This value also makes the <probability density function> nonnegative everywhere.

= ii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}
{c}

Obtain each <marginal density> by integrating the <joint probability density function> over the other variable:
$$
\boxed{f_U(u)=\int_0^1u(1+2v)\,dv=2u,\quad0\le u\le1,}
$$
$$
\boxed{f_V(v)=\int_0^1u(1+2v)\,du=\frac12+v,\quad0\le v\le1.}
$$
Each <marginal density> is zero off its displayed <interval> and has total <integral> one.

= iii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}
{c}

On the product support $[0,1]^2$, the <joint probability density function> factors as
$$
\boxed{f_{U,V}(u,v)=u(1+2v)=(2u)(1/2+v)=f_U(u)f_V(v).}
$$
The equality also holds outside the support with both sides zero. The <probability density function> factorization criterion proves that **$U$ and $V$ are <independent random variables>**.

= b
{parent=6}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}
{c}

For each nonnegative integer $z$, the <events> $\{X=z-y,Y=y\}$, $0\le y\le z$, are pairwise disjoint and their union is $\{X+Y=z\}$. By <countable additivity> and then <independence>,
$$
\begin{aligned}
p_Z(z)&=\sum_{y=0}^z\mathbb P(X=z-y,Y=y)\\
&=\boxed{\sum_{y=0}^zp_Y(y)p_X(z-y).}
\end{aligned}
$$
This is the <discrete convolution> of the two <probability mass functions>. If the convention for natural numbers excludes zero, set both <probability mass functions> equal to zero there; the formula remains valid. Also $p_Z(z)=0$ for negative $z$.

= c
{parent=6}
