= i
{parent=c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}
{c}

Let $R$ denote the red die. Conditional on $R=r$, each white roll succeeds independently with <probability> $p_r=(7-r)/6$. Consequently $T\mid R=r$ has the <geometric distribution> on $\{1,2,\ldots\}$ and <expected value> $1/p_r=6/(7-r)$. The <law of total expectation> gives
$$
\boxed{\mathbb E T=\frac16\sum_{r=1}^6\frac6{7-r}
=\sum_{k=1}^6\frac1k=\frac{49}{20}.}
$$
This is $2.45$ rolls; the averaging must be over conditional <expected values>, not over success <probabilities> before taking a reciprocal.

= ii
{parent=c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}
{c}

By the <law of total probability>,
$$
\mathbb P(T=1)=\frac16\sum_{r=1}^6\frac{7-r}{6}=\frac7{12}.
$$
<Bayes' theorem> therefore gives the <conditional probability mass function>
$$
\boxed{\mathbb P(R=r\mid T=1)
=\frac{(1/6)(7-r)/6}{7/12}=\frac{7-r}{21},\quad r=1,\ldots,6.}
$$
The six <probabilities> sum to one. A small red outcome is more likely after an immediate success because more white faces can meet its threshold.

= iii
{parent=c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}
{c}

For a <geometric distribution> on positive integers, the <probability generating function> is $p s/[1-(1-p)s]$. Conditional averaging over the red die yields
$$
\boxed{G_T(s)=\frac16\sum_{r=1}^6\frac{(7-r)s}{6-(r-1)s}
=\frac16\sum_{k=1}^6\frac{ks}{6-(6-k)s}.}
$$
As a <power series>, its exact domain of absolute convergence is
$$
\boxed{|s|<\frac65.}
$$
Indeed the slowest decaying component has failure <probability> $5/6$, so $\mathbb P(T=t)\ge(1/36)(5/6)^{t-1}$. At $|s|=6/5$, the terms of the <power series> fail to tend to zero; outside that disk they grow. All six component series converge inside it. In particular, the <probability generating function> is defined on the usual real <interval> $0\le s\le1$, with $G_T(1)=1$. The rational expression beyond the convergence disk is only an algebraic continuation, not the defining <expected value>.

= 7
{parent=Mathematics III resit}
{scope}

= a
{parent=7}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}
{c}

The <least squares estimators> choose the intercept and slope to minimize the sum of squared <regression residuals>:
$$
\boxed{(\widehat\alpha,\widehat\beta)\in
\operatorname*{arg\,min}_{a,b\in\mathbb R}\sum_{i=1}^n(Y_i-a-bx_i)^2.}
$$
After observation, replace $Y_i$ by $y_i$ to obtain the numerical <estimates>. Their definition does not require a <normal distribution> for the errors. A unique slope requires some variation in the fixed predictors; under $\sum_i x_i=0$, this is $S_{xx}=\sum_i x_i^2>0$, assumed below. If all $x_i=0$, the slope is unidentifiable.

= b
{parent=7}
{scope}

= i
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}
{c}

Differentiate the <ordinary least squares> objective in <7/a>. Its <normal equations for linear least squares> are
$$
\sum_i(Y_i-\widehat\alpha-\widehat\beta x_i)=0,\qquad
\sum_i x_i(Y_i-\widehat\alpha-\widehat\beta x_i)=0.
$$
Using $\sum_i x_i=0$ and $S_{xx}=\sum_i x_i^2>0$ gives
$$
\boxed{\widehat\alpha=\overline Y,\qquad
\widehat\beta=\frac{\sum_i x_iY_i}{S_{xx}}.}
$$
The <Hessian matrix> of the objective in $(a,b)$ is $2\operatorname{diag}(n,S_{xx})$, which is <positive definite>, so this is its unique <global minimum>. For the observed <estimates>, replace $Y_i$ by $y_i$.

= ii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}
{c}

The centered design gives $\widehat\beta=\beta+S_{xx}^{-1}\sum_i x_i\epsilon_i$. By <independence> of the errors, their off-diagonal <covariances> vanish. Thus <variance additivity for independent random variables> gives
$$
\boxed{\operatorname{Var}(\widehat\beta)
=\frac{\sum_i x_i^2\sigma^2}{S_{xx}^2}
=\frac{\sigma^2}{S_{xx}}.}
$$
A larger spread of predictor values decreases the slope <variance> for fixed error <variance>.

= iii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}
{c}

Here $\widehat\alpha=\alpha+n^{-1}\sum_i\epsilon_i$. By <linearity of covariance> and <independence>,
$$
\boxed{\operatorname{Cov}(\widehat\alpha,\widehat\beta)
=\frac1{nS_{xx}}\sum_{i,j}x_j\operatorname{Cov}(\epsilon_i,\epsilon_j)
=\frac{\sigma^2}{nS_{xx}}\sum_i x_i=0.}
$$
Thus the two <estimators> are <uncorrelated random variables>.

= iv
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iv}
{c}

**They need not be <independent>.** For a counterexample take $n=2$, $x_1=-1$, $x_2=1$, and independent errors taking $-1,1$ with equal <probability>. Then
$$
\widehat\alpha-\alpha=\frac{\epsilon_1+\epsilon_2}{2},\qquad
\widehat\beta-\beta=\frac{\epsilon_2-\epsilon_1}{2}.
$$
Each centered <estimator> is zero with <probability> $1/2$, but they are never both zero. Their zero <events> therefore violate <independence>.

A sufficient additional assumption is that the errors have a common <normal distribution>. The two <estimators> are then <jointly Gaussian>, being linear combinations of the independent <Gaussian random variables>. Their zero <covariance>, established in <7/b/iii>, implies <independence of uncorrelated jointly normal variables>. Normal errors are a sufficient condition here, not a claim that no other error <probability distribution> could ever work.

= c
{parent=7}
{scope}

= i
{parent=c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}
{c}

For the <random vector> $\boldsymbol\epsilon=(\epsilon_1,\ldots,\epsilon_n)^\top$, its <covariance matrix> is
$$
\boxed{\Sigma=\mathbb E[(\boldsymbol\epsilon-\mathbb E\boldsymbol\epsilon)
(\boldsymbol\epsilon-\mathbb E\boldsymbol\epsilon)^\top],\qquad
\Sigma_{ij}=\operatorname{Cov}(\epsilon_i,\epsilon_j).}
$$
Its diagonal entries are <variances>, and its off-diagonal entries measure pairwise <covariance>. Finite <second moments> make every entry well defined.

= ii
{parent=c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}
{c}

<Independence> of the centered <standard normal random variables> gives $\mathbb E\epsilon_i=0$ for every $i$. Also
$$
\mathbb E\epsilon_1^2=1,\qquad
\mathbb E\epsilon_i^2=\mathbb E\eta_i^2\,\mathbb E\eta_{i-1}^2=1\quad(i\ge2).
$$
For nonadjacent indices, the underlying sets of $\eta$ variables are disjoint, so the errors are <independent> and have zero <covariance>. For adjacent indices with $i\ge2$,
$$
\mathbb E[\epsilon_i\epsilon_{i+1}]
=\mathbb E[\eta_{i-1}\eta_i^2\eta_{i+1}]
=\mathbb E\eta_{i-1}\,\mathbb E\eta_i^2\,\mathbb E\eta_{i+1}=0.
$$
The first adjacent pair separately gives $\mathbb E[\epsilon_1\epsilon_2]=\mathbb E[\eta_1^2\eta_2]=0$. Hence
$$
\boxed{\Sigma=I_n.}
$$
These <adjacent products of independent Gaussian variables> are <uncorrelated random variables> even though adjacent errors share a factor and are dependent.

= iii
{parent=c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}
{c}

The <least squares estimators> depend only on the observed responses and design, so the formulas in <7/b/i> still hold. Their <variances> and <covariance> need only the error <covariance matrix>, now $I_n$. Therefore
$$
\boxed{\operatorname{Var}(\widehat\beta)=\frac1{S_{xx}},\qquad
\operatorname{Cov}(\widehat\alpha,\widehat\beta)=0.}
$$
For example the first formula follows from $\operatorname{Var}(\sum_i x_i\epsilon_i)=\sum_i x_i^2$, and the second from $\sum_i x_i=0$. The formulas remain the same as before with $\sigma^2=1$; this calculation does not establish <independence> or a <normal distribution> for the <estimators>.

= iv
{parent=c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iv}
{c}

Compute fitted responses $\widehat y_i=\widehat\alpha+\widehat\beta x_i$ and <regression residuals> $e_i=y_i-\widehat y_i$. A conventional <residual plot> shows $e_i$ against $\widehat y_i$ or $x_i$; plotting against observation order additionally looks for runs. One checks for curvature, changing spread and other structure rather than random scatter around zero.

**An ordinary <residual plot> need not reveal this failure of <independence>.** The errors have constant <mean> zero, constant <variance> one and zero pairwise <covariances>, so the usual trend and changing-<variance> clues need not appear. A visually patternless <residual plot> cannot validate <independence>; fitted <regression residuals> themselves also share the fitted coefficients even when the true errors are <independent>.

There is detectable dependence in the magnitudes. The <Gaussian fourth moment> is $\mathbb E\eta_i^4=3$, giving for every adjacent pair
$$
\boxed{\operatorname{Cov}(\epsilon_i^2,\epsilon_{i+1}^2)=3-1=2.}
$$
For $i=1$, the product is $\eta_1^4\eta_2^2$; for $i\ge2$, it is $\eta_{i-1}^2\eta_i^4\eta_{i+1}^2$, with the same <expected value> 3. Large magnitudes tend to occur together. A lagged plot of squared <regression residuals> or a test directed at dependence in their squares is more informative. Thus recognition is possible with suitable diagnostics, but is not guaranteed by the standard <residual plot> requested here.

= 8
{parent=Mathematics III resit}
{scope}

= a
{parent=8}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}
{c}

For observations $\mathbf x_i\in\mathbb R^p$, <K-means clustering> seeks nonempty <clusters in cluster analysis> $C_1,\ldots,C_K$ and <centroids> $\mathbf m_k$ minimizing
$$
\boxed{W=\sum_{k=1}^K\sum_{i\in C_k}\|\mathbf x_i-\mathbf m_k\|^2,\qquad
\mathbf m_k=\frac1{|C_k|}\sum_{i\in C_k}\mathbf x_i.}
$$
At fixed <centroids>, assigning each point to a nearest <centroid> minimizes its squared <Euclidean distance>. At fixed assignments, the <centroid sum-of-squares identity> shows that the <sample mean> uniquely minimizes each group's contribution. These alternating steps cannot increase $W$.

For <finite termination of K-means clustering>, more than a lower bound on $W$ is needed. Keep an assignment unchanged when nearest <centroids> tie, and stop when assignments no longer change. Every change then strictly decreases $W$. After updating the <centroids>, $W$ is determined by the <partition of a set> of $n$ indices; there are only finitely many choices for that <partition of a set>, so no strictly improving <partition of a set> can recur. Empty <clusters in cluster analysis> require a convention, such as moving a point from a nonsingleton <cluster in cluster analysis> to become its own <centroid>, which cannot increase $W$. With such a consistent convention the algorithm terminates, although it may reach only a <local minimum>.

= b
{parent=8}
{scope}

= i
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}
{c}

Every <Euclidean distance> obeys the <triangle inequality>. The three recorded entries $d_{14}=0.67$, $d_{16}=2.88$ and $d_{46}=4.37$ violate it:
$$
\boxed{d_{46}=4.37>0.67+2.88=3.55=d_{41}+d_{16}.}
$$
**At least one of $0.67,\ 2.88,\ 4.37$ must be incorrect.** Two are in the final row as required. The gap is far larger than any possible rounding of entries to two decimal places.

= ii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}
{c}

<Complete-linkage clustering> defines the dissimilarity between <clusters in cluster analysis> $A,B$ as $d_{\rm CL}(A,B)=\max_{i\in A,j\in B}d_{ij}$. Remove point 6 and first merge 1 with 4 at height $0.67$. The distances from $\{1,4\}$ to 2, 3, 5 are respectively
$$
\max(1.41,1.19)=1.41,\quad
\max(2.68,2.19)=2.68,\quad
\max(3.01,3.32)=3.32.
$$
The next merge therefore attaches 2 at $1.41$. Now the distances from $\{1,2,4\}$ to 3 and 5 are $2.95$ and $3.32$, whereas $d_{35}=2.82$. Merge 3 and 5 at $2.82$. The final distance between the two remaining <clusters in cluster analysis> is the largest cross-entry, $3.32$. Thus the <dendrogram> has merges
$$
\boxed{(1,4):0.67,\quad(\{1,4\},2):1.41,\quad(3,5):2.82,\quad
(\{1,2,4\},\{3,5\}):3.32.}
$$
\Image[mathematics-iii-resit-complete-linkage.png]
{title=Complete-linkage dendrogram after removing observation 6}

Cutting strictly between heights $2.82$ and $3.32$ gives the two <clusters in cluster analysis>
$$
\boxed{\{1,2,4\}\quad\text{and}\quad\{3,5\}.}
$$
The figure is an original drawing of these computed merges.

= c
{parent=8}
{scope}

= i
{parent=c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}
{c}

With two distinct <centroids>, <K-means clustering> assigns points on opposite sides of their <nearest-centroid decision hyperplane>, which in two dimensions is a straight <line>. This constraint explains the typical outcomes in the three source plots; exact assignments depend on initialization and the realized points.

**Dataset A:** one <cluster in cluster analysis> contains most of the horizontal cloud and the bottom of the vertical cloud; the other contains the upper part of the vertical cloud. The two visually elongated groups are poorly matched to squared <Euclidean distance> from their <centroids>. Points at the lower end of the vertical group can be closer to the horizontal group's <centroid>, so the two original shapes need not be recovered intact.

**Dataset B:** a roughly straight cut divides the ring and generally the central cloud between the two <clusters in cluster analysis>. A <Voronoi cell> for either <centroid> is a <half-plane>; it cannot select a surrounding ring while excluding its center. The cut's direction can vary because the geometry is approximately rotationally symmetric.

**Dataset C:** the two dense lower clouds typically receive different <centroids>. The much smaller upper cloud is absorbed into one or divided between both <clusters in cluster analysis>, according to the nearly vertical separating <line>. With $K=2$, the objective usually benefits more from separating the two large clouds than from spending one <centroid> on the sparse third group. These are typical qualitative outcomes, rather than guarantees about every initialization.

= ii
{parent=c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}
{c}

**It is <single-linkage clustering>.** Its intercluster distance is the smallest cross-pair <Euclidean distance>. Successive nearby points can join around the ring and within the dense central cloud before any edge crosses the gap between them. Equivalently, at a threshold below the between-group gap but above the lengths needed to connect each group, the <single-linkage clustering> components are precisely the central group and the ring.

<Complete-linkage clustering> uses the largest cross-pair <Euclidean distance>. Opposite points on the ring are far apart, so an intact ring has a large <diameter>. That rule favors compact pieces and can attach a ring segment to the center before all ring segments join. The visible gap and short links within each group explain why <single-linkage clustering> recovers the two intended <clusters in cluster analysis> in this example; it is not a universal guarantee for arbitrary noisy rings.

= 9
{parent=Mathematics III resit}
{scope}

= a
{parent=9}
{scope}

= i
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}
{c}

The independent uniform choices from the student list describe <sampling with replacement>. Under the stated independent <normal distribution> model for the heights, the population <variance> is unknown and the given $8.5$ cm is the <sample standard deviation> $s$. The exact <pivotal quantity> is
$$
\frac{\overline X-\mu}{S/\sqrt{100}}\sim t_{99}.
$$
Thus the exact 95% <Student t confidence interval> is
$$
\boxed{171.7\pm t_{99,0.975}\frac{8.5}{10}\ \mathrm{cm}
\ \approx\ [170.01,173.39]\ \mathrm{cm},}
$$
using $t_{99,0.975}\approx1.9842$. The supplied approximation $\Phi(2)=0.975$ instead gives a <normal approximation> for large <sample size>
$$
\boxed{171.7\pm2(0.85)=[170.0,173.4]\ \mathrm{cm}.}
$$
This latter <confidence interval> is approximate even under <normality> because it substitutes the <sample standard deviation> for the unknown population value. There is no <finite population correction> under the specified independent selections. Interpreting the finite student list literally as a discrete population makes <normality> an idealized model; it does not justify changing the stated sampling design.

= ii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}
{c}

At <sample size> 100, keep essentially the same numerical <confidence interval>, but treat its calibration as approximate. For independent heights with finite, nonzero <variance>, the <central limit theorem> makes the <sample mean> approximately <normally distributed>, and the <sample variance> consistently estimates the population <variance>. Hence the <studentized sample mean> is approximately standard normal even without a <normal distribution> for individual heights.

Using the permitted cutoff 2 gives
$$
\boxed{\mu\approx\overline x\pm2\frac{s}{\sqrt n}
=[170.0,173.4]\ \mathrm{cm}\quad\text{at approximately 95\% confidence}.}
$$
A <Student t-distribution> cutoff gives a similar approximation for large <sample size>, but its justification at fixed <sample size> is no longer exact. Extreme skewness or influential <outliers> could make 100 observations insufficient for accurate <coverage probability>; abandoning <normality> alone supplies no specific numerical correction. The <sampling with replacement> assumption still precludes a <finite population correction>.

= b
{parent=9}
{scope}

= i
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}
{c}

For this rate parametrization of the <Laplace distribution>, $\mu$ is its <mean> and <median>. By <symmetry>, $\mathbb E(X-\mu)=0$, while $|X-\mu|$ has <exponential distribution> with rate $\lambda$, so
$$
\operatorname{Var}(X)=\mathbb E|X-\mu|^2
=\int_0^\infty r^2\lambda e^{-\lambda r}\,dr=\frac2{\lambda^2}.
$$
The <integral> follows by two applications of <integration by parts>. The <central limit theorem> therefore gives
$$
\frac{\sqrt n(\overline X-\mu)}{\sqrt2/\lambda}
\ \xrightarrow{d}\ N(0,1).
$$
With known $\lambda$ and the supplied cutoff 2, an approximate 95% <confidence interval> is
$$
\boxed{\left[\overline x-\frac{2\sqrt2}{\lambda\sqrt n},\ 
\overline x+\frac{2\sqrt2}{\lambda\sqrt n}\right].}
$$
This <known-rate Laplace location confidence interval> uses the <sample mean>; the later <maximum likelihood estimator> of location need not be used to construct this valid asymptotic <confidence interval>.

= ii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}
{c}

By <independence>, the <likelihood function> is the product of the individual <probability density functions>:
$$
\boxed{L(\mu,\lambda;x_1,\ldots,x_n)
=\left(\frac\lambda2\right)^n
\exp\!\left[-\lambda\sum_{i=1}^n|x_i-\mu|\right],\quad
\mu\in\mathbb R,\ \lambda>0.}
$$
Writing $A(\mu)=\sum_i|x_i-\mu|$, the <log-likelihood function> is $\ell=n\ln\lambda-n\ln2-\lambda A(\mu)$. Here $\lambda$ is a rate, the reciprocal of the scale in the usual <Laplace distribution> notation.

= iii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}
{c}

The joint <maximum likelihood estimators> are functions of the observations selecting a maximizing parameter pair:
$$
\boxed{(\widehat\mu,\widehat\lambda)\in
\operatorname*{arg\,max}_{\mu\in\mathbb R,\lambda>0}
L(\mu,\lambda;X_1,\ldots,X_n).}
$$
The <estimates> after observation maximize the same <likelihood function> with $X_i=x_i$. Maximization may be nonunique, as for an even <sample size>, or may fail to yield a finite admissible pair, as in the one-observation case below. The definition does not turn a boundary supremum into an attained <maximum>.

= iv
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iv}
{c}

For each fixed $\lambda>0$, maximizing the <likelihood function> over $\mu$ is equivalent to minimizing $A(\mu)=\sum_i|x_i-\mu|$. Order the distinct observations as $x_{(1)}<\cdots<x_{(n)}$ and write $n=2m+1$. Between successive <order statistics>, with exactly $k$ observations to the left,
$$
A'(\mu)=k-(n-k)=2k-n.
$$
Thus $A$ decreases up to $x_{(m+1)}$ and increases after it. Its unique <minimum> is at the <sample median>:
$$
\boxed{\widehat\mu=x_{(m+1)}.}
$$
At a fixed $\mu$ with $A(\mu)>0$, the <log-likelihood function> has <derivative> $n/\lambda-A(\mu)$ and second <derivative> $-n/\lambda^2<0$, so its unique maximizing rate is $n/A(\mu)$. The maximized <likelihood function> is $(n/[2eA(\mu)])^n$, which increases when $A(\mu)$ decreases. Hence the joint <maximum likelihood estimates> are
$$
\boxed{\widehat\mu=x_{(m+1)},\qquad
\widehat\lambda=\frac{n}{\sum_i|x_i-x_{(m+1)}|}.}
$$
This is <Laplace location and scale maximum likelihood>, expressed with the reciprocal scale.

**A finite rate <maximum likelihood estimate> requires a positive total absolute deviation.** For distinct observations and odd $n\ge3$, that condition holds. For $n=1$, however, $\mu=x_1$ gives $A(\mu)=0$ and $L=\lambda/2\to\infty$ as $\lambda\to\infty$. There is no finite joint <maximum likelihood estimate> in the stated parameter space; the formula with zero denominator is not an admissible estimate.

= v
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=v}
{c}

Let $n=2m\ge2$. Between the two middle <order statistics>, $k=m$ and the slope $A'(\mu)=2m-n$ is zero. To the left $A$ decreases and to the right it increases. Consequently all joint <maximum likelihood estimates> are
$$
\boxed{\widehat\mu\in[x_{(m)},x_{(m+1)}],\qquad
\widehat\lambda=\frac{n}{A_{\min}},}
$$
where the constant minimum is
$$
\boxed{A_{\min}=\sum_{i=m+1}^{2m}x_{(i)}-\sum_{i=1}^mx_{(i)}>0.}
$$
The strict positivity follows from distinct observations. Thus the location <maximum likelihood estimate> is not unique, while the rate <maximum likelihood estimate> is unique and the same for every location in the displayed <interval>. Choosing the arithmetic midpoint of the two middle values is the usual <sample median> convention and gives one member of this entire maximizing family.
